1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,652
Now part two, feature selection.

2
00:00:02,652 --> 00:00:07,542
The goal is a smaller set of columns that predicts as well:

3
00:00:07,542 --> 00:00:12,349
simpler, faster, less over-fitting, and easier to explain.

4
00:00:12,349 --> 00:00:14,421
There are three families.

5
00:00:14,421 --> 00:00:20,223
Filter methods score each feature before the model and keep the top k.

6
00:00:20,223 --> 00:00:26,107
Wrapper methods try subsets around the model, train, score, and repeat.

7
00:00:26,107 --> 00:00:31,992
Embedded methods select inside the model, with a penalty or importance.

8
00:00:32,792 --> 00:00:36,611
Filter methods score each feature on its own.

9
00:00:36,611 --> 00:00:41,024
Variance threshold removes almost-constant features.

10
00:00:41,024 --> 00:00:46,879
Pearson correlation keeps large absolute correlation with the target.

11
00:00:46,879 --> 00:00:52,735
The A N O V A F-test suits numeric features and a categorical target.

12
00:00:52,735 --> 00:00:55,959
Chi-square suits categorical features.

13
00:00:55,959 --> 00:00:59,269
Mutual information works for any types.

14
00:00:59,269 --> 00:01:08,943
Filters are very fast, model-free, and a good first screen, but they ignore interactions and keep near-duplicates.

15
00:01:08,943 --> 00:01:09,622
Caution:

16
00:01:09,622 --> 00:01:16,496
variance depends on scale, so tiny-valued columns look constant even when useful.

17
00:01:17,296 --> 00:01:18,713
A filter example:

18
00:01:18,713 --> 00:01:21,131
A N O V A F-score, top eight.

19
00:01:21,131 --> 00:01:37,138
Log page values, has page value, page values, and the two page value interaction terms are all versions of the same signal, and a filter scores each feature alone so it happily keeps all five.

20
00:01:37,138 --> 00:01:42,640
Month November never makes the list, though models find it useful.

21
00:01:43,440 --> 00:01:46,335
Wrapper methods let the model choose.

22
00:01:46,335 --> 00:01:52,672
Forward selection starts empty and adds the feature that improves the score most.

23
00:01:52,672 --> 00:01:59,243
Backward elimination starts with all and removes the feature whose loss hurts least.

24
00:01:59,243 --> 00:02:07,066
Recursive feature elimination fits, drops the feature with the smallest weight, refits, and repeats.

25
00:02:07,066 --> 00:02:08,083
The strength:

26
00:02:08,083 --> 00:02:14,107
they see interactions and redundancy, and usually find the best small subset.

27
00:02:14,107 --> 00:02:15,124
The weakness:

28
00:02:15,124 --> 00:02:21,696
they are slow, training the model hundreds of times, and can over-fit the selection.

29
00:02:22,496 --> 00:02:25,573
Embedded methods select while training.

30
00:02:25,573 --> 00:02:36,223
L one, or Lasso, adds a penalty that pushes weak coefficients to exactly zero; a smaller C means a stronger penalty and fewer features.

31
00:02:36,223 --> 00:02:44,033
Here, twenty of eighty five features are kept, with log page values and has page value the largest.

32
00:02:44,033 --> 00:02:47,110
Tree importance is the second approach:

33
00:02:47,110 --> 00:02:54,368
random forests record how much each feature splits the data well, and you keep the top ones.

34
00:02:55,168 --> 00:02:56,604
Compare the three.

35
00:02:56,604 --> 00:03:08,015
Filter uses a statistic, is fastest, does not see interactions, and its main risk is keeping duplicates; it is a first screen of many features.

36
00:03:08,015 --> 00:03:19,985
Wrapper trains many times, is slowest, sees interactions and redundancy, and its risk is over-fitting the selection; use it to fine-tune a short list.

37
00:03:19,985 --> 00:03:29,560
Embedded trains once, is medium speed, partly sees interactions, is tied to one model type, and is the everyday default.

38
00:03:30,360 --> 00:03:34,445
A practical selection workflow in four steps.

39
00:03:34,445 --> 00:03:34,989
Clean:

40
00:03:34,989 --> 00:03:38,257
drop constant and duplicate columns.

41
00:03:38,257 --> 00:03:38,892
Screen:

42
00:03:38,892 --> 00:03:44,429
filter to a shortlist with correlation or mutual information.

43
00:03:44,429 --> 00:03:45,064
Choose:

44
00:03:45,064 --> 00:03:49,149
embedded L one or a wrapper on the shortlist.

45
00:03:49,149 --> 00:03:49,693
Check:

46
00:03:49,693 --> 00:03:52,053
score on unseen test rows.

47
00:03:52,053 --> 00:03:53,506
The golden rule:

48
00:03:53,506 --> 00:03:57,590
select features using the training rows only.

49
00:03:57,590 --> 00:04:04,670
If the test rows help choose the features, the test score is no longer honest.

50
00:04:04,670 --> 00:04:06,576
That is data leakage.

51
00:04:07,376 --> 00:04:08,592
Key takeaways.

52
00:04:08,592 --> 00:04:10,589
Construct and interact:

53
00:04:10,589 --> 00:04:16,320
build features that state the pattern directly, and test each one.

54
00:04:16,320 --> 00:04:25,003
Polynomial features grow as C of n plus d choose d minus one, so use low degree, scale, then select.

55
00:04:25,003 --> 00:04:31,168
Too many columns means a sparse space, weak distances and over-fitting.

56
00:04:31,168 --> 00:04:39,503
And filter is a fast screen, wrapper gives the best small set, embedded is the everyday default.

57
00:04:39,503 --> 00:04:41,240
Thanks for watching.

